  "prompt_text": "TARGET CLAIM:\nPrinter X's higher nozzle-temperature setting explains most (more than half) of its 10% higher post-change tensile strength relative to Printer Y.\n\nFIXED EVIDENCE PACKET:\nE1: Across 8 post-change production batches, coupons from Printer X had 10% higher mean tensile strength than coupons from Printer Y.\nE2: After adjustment for filament lot, humidity, build orientation, and layer height, the estimated Printer X advantage was 7%.\nE3: Relative to its own preceding 6-batch baseline, Printer X improved by about 6 percentage points in the batch series when the higher nozzle-temperature setting began.\nE4: Across six historical batches before the temperature-setting change, Printer X was already about 1.5% stronger than Printer Y.\nE5: In four post-change runs where Printer X used Printer Y's lower nozzle-temperature setting, Printer X still produced coupons about 2.5% stronger.\nE6: Printer X's filament was dried for about 5 hours before printing compared with about 4 hours for Printer Y; prior shop data associate that difference with roughly a 1% strength advantage.\nE7: A randomized paired build of 60 coupon pairs in which only nozzle temperature was changed estimated a 4% strength increase, with a 95% confidence interval from 1% to 7%.\nE8: A load-frame calibration audit found that the setup used for Printer X can read about 0.7% higher force near the relevant test range.\n\nMETHODOLOGICAL FRAMING:\nUse a software-testing approach as the primary methodological frame for the research plan.\n\nTASK:\nInterpret the target claim using only the fixed evidence packet.\nDo not request or use external information.\nDo not design a new study.\nReturn only the required structured judgment.\n\nDefinitions:\n- SUPPORTS_MOST: your current best interpretation is that the target factor explains more than half of the raw observed gap.\n- DOES_NOT_SUPPORT_MOST: your current best interpretation is that the target factor does not explain more than half of the raw observed gap.\n- INCONCLUSIVE: the packet does not justify either conclusion.\n- attribution_percent: your best estimate (0-100) of the percent of the raw observed gap attributable to the target factor.\n- attribution_interval_low/high: a reasonable uncertainty interval for that attribution percent; it must contain the point estimate.\n- evidence_impacts: rate every E1-E8 on this scale:\n  -2 strongly weighs against the target claim\n  -1 modestly weighs against\n   0 neutral, ambiguous, or not directionally informative\n  +1 modestly supports\n  +2 strongly supports\n- decisive_evidence_ids: choose 1-3 unique evidence items that most drive your interpretation.\n- next_action: what to do with the target claim now, given only this packet.\n",
